From e0c8823c96304036b54ef302f0c788164179739b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:44:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix sign extension when folding loads of signed values from
 known bytes

LoadConstantFold and LoadConstantOperand assembled the bytes of an 8 or
16 bit value from constant initializer data as an unsigned number, and
the IC_FILL case of LoadStoreForwarding did the same with the memset
fill byte.  A signed struct member initialized to -3 was therefore
folded to the constant 65533 once the optimizer read a local struct
through its initializer, a loop over a const int table evaluated at
compile time summed to 65533 instead of -3, and an int read back from
memset(0xff) was forwarded as 65535 instead of -1.  The stored bytes
were right; the constant folding and value range analysis that follow
then worked with a value outside the type's range: pt->x < 0 ? -pt->x :
pt->x was folded to -65533, a comparison of the result with 3 was folded
to false, and s / 3 was evaluated at compile time to 21844 instead of -1.

Sign extend the folded value unless the load is known to be
non-negative, and make the 32 bit case explicit the same way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
